Mr. J. P. JAFPRAT.

There are two authorities over those elevators. Those elevators can serve but one purpose, namely, to store the country's produce which is bought for export, and then to turn it over to the steamer that is to take it overseas, or the freight oars that are to take it elsewhere. The operations of those elevators should be uniform, and you cannot obtain the same degree of efficiency so far as uniformity is concerned, by having two departments controlling them, as by having only one.
Census and statistics, $100,000.
)Mr. BUREAU: Is this for the new and famous department that has been inaugurated this session?

We have several commissions whose duties seem to be similar to those of the Conservation Commission. We have a commission on natural resources, we have another commission to inquire into such resources as ores, etc., which *might he manufactured into munitions. In fact, there is such a multiplicity of commissions that the ordinary public, if they want information, do not know which one .to apply to. The Conservation Commission may he looked upon as the mother of commissions, if we leave out of consideration the Railway Commission.. I suggest that the Government charge the Conservation Commission with the duty of studying the other
commissions and ascertaining whether some of them could not be dispensed with or merged with others. Most of the information, if we except some minute details, supplied by the commission mentioned yesterday could be obtained from the various departments of Government. I do not make these remarks by way of criticism;
I admit that the Government have had a good deal to do all at once. But now that we have Union Government-
